From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 20 Aug 2026 18:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] media: cix: amvx: drain both workqueues before destroying the if layer

mvx_dev_remove() previously destroyed ctx->work_queue and sched->sched_queue
after mvx_if_destroy(), which left a window where both queues could still
drain work items that referenced sched->if_ops after the if context had
already been freed:

  irq_bottom (ctx->work_queue) -> mvx_sched_handle_irq -> sched->if_ops->irq()
  sched_task (sched_queue)    -> sched->if_ops->irq()

mvx_if_destroy() kobject_put()s ctx->kobj and then waits for the if_release
completion; once it returns, the if context backing sched->if_ops has been
kfree()d. Any work item that runs after that point dereferences freed memory.

This UAF can present as a shutdown hang when the freed slab is reused for
something the UAF writer then blocks on (e.g. a struct containing a mutex
that another code path tries to acquire), which is consistent with the
shutdown-hang symptom being root-caused on a parallel series.

Drain ctx->work_queue and sched->sched_queue immediately after free_irq()
so that no further work items can run once mvx_if_destroy() begins. The
mvx_hwreg_destruct() MMIO release remains the last step. mvx_sched_destruct()
is safe to call here -- mvx_lsid_destruct() is empty in this tree, and the
scheduler's only other resource is its own workqueue.

Also clear ctx->work_queue after destroy so any later reference (in the
failure path) sees NULL rather than a freed pointer.

Not yet hardware-tested; needs an Orion O6 shutdown test on real hardware
before it can be trusted to fix the hang seen by the third party.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c b/drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/cix/dev/mvx_dev.c
@@ -1031,6 +1031,28 @@
     int i;
 
     free_irq(ctx->irq, ctx);
+
+    /*
+     * Drain ctx->work_queue and the scheduler's workqueue before tearing
+     * down the if layer.  Both queues run items that dereference
+     * sched->if_ops (the IRQ bottom-half calls mvx_sched_handle_irq ->
+     * sched->if_ops->irq(); sched_task fires sched->if_ops->irq() on
+     * every entry in its notify_list).  mvx_if_destroy() releases the
+     * last reference on ctx->kobj and then kfree()s the if context
+     * backing sched->if_ops, so any work item that runs after that
+     * dereferences freed memory.  In practice this UAF can present as a
+     * shutdown hang when the freed slab is reused for something that the
+     * UAF writer then blocks on (e.g. a struct containing a mutex that
+     * some other code path tries to acquire).
+     *
+     * mvx_sched_destruct() is a no-op on the lsids (mvx_lsid_destruct is
+     * empty) and only destroys sched->sched_queue, so it is safe to run
+     * here ahead of the devfreq / runtime-PM teardown that follows.
+     */
+    destroy_workqueue(ctx->work_queue);
+    ctx->work_queue = NULL;
+    mvx_sched_destruct(&ctx->scheduler);
+
     mvx_devfreq_remove(ctx);
     if (has_acpi_companion(ctx->dev)) {
 #if VPU_CORE_ACPI_REF_POWERSOURCE
@@ -1045,8 +1067,6 @@
 
     MVX_LOG_PRINT(&mvx_log_dev, MVX_LOG_INFO, "remove");
     mvx_if_destroy(ctx->if_ops);
-    destroy_workqueue(ctx->work_queue);
-    mvx_sched_destruct(&ctx->scheduler);
     mvx_hwreg_destruct(&ctx->hwreg);
     dev_set_drvdata(ctx->dev, NULL);
     if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_DEBUG_FS))
-- 
2.51.0
